WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.740
Universal plans to launch a monster movie
universe on the back of Tom Cruise's The Mummy.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:11.190
The flick serves as a reboot of both the studio's
classic scary movie franchise and the Brendan

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.610
Fraser-starring trilogy.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:18.420
However, despite lofty expectations and the
attachment of a huge star in Cruise, The Mummy

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:23.300
wasn't able to scare up many viewers in its
opening weekend, debuting to an underwhelming

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.050
$31.5 million domestic gross.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:29.920
As it turned out, nobody but Cruise said this:

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.561
"Show me the Mummy!"

00:00:31.561 --> 00:00:36.269
The disappointing start for the thriller likely
won't completely unravel Universal's so-called

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:39.899
"Dark Universe," but it definitely spells
trouble.

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:43.160
Here's why The Mummy was dead on arrival.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.329
Critical curse

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:49.499
The Mummy was absolutely trashed by critics,
quickly falling below a 20 percent on Rotten

00:00:49.499 --> 00:00:52.300
Tomatoes during its debut weekend.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.850
Indiewire called the film "an irredeemable
disaster from start to finish, an adventure

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:00.550
that entertains only via glimpses of the adventure
it should have been."

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:05.960
Vulture said it hits its marks — but they're
"so low and so brazenly mercenary that it

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.880
doesn't feel like much of an achievement."

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.230
Rolling Stone quipped that it's "a monster
fail," while The A.V.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:15.890
Club said it was "noisy but rarely exciting,
heavy on whizzing and whirling special effects

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.380
but light on wonder."

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:18.760
"Kick her ass!"

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.000
It's hard to find anything positive critics
have said about the movie — even those who

00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:31.140
gave it "fresh" reviews mostly just deemed
it tolerable, rather than actually entertaining.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:35.521
With so many other options out there, viewers
have little reason to head out to a movie

00:01:35.521 --> 00:01:38.270
that critics had so thoroughly buried.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:40.130
Wrongheaded reboot

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:46.189
The classic 1932 The Mummy is a far, far cry
from the 2017 version — something classic

00:01:46.189 --> 00:01:50.149
cinema fans surely noticed when the trailer
for the new film dropped.

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:55.490
Starring Boris Karloff as the namesake monster,
the original flick relied on quieter, atmospheric

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:59.429
horror that kept viewers on the edge of their
seats without aggressively trying to scare

00:01:59.429 --> 00:02:01.009
them at every turn.

00:02:01.009 --> 00:02:05.630
The new movie, on the other hand, goes for
near-constant CGI thrills that are more visual

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:08.310
surprises than actual scares.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.910
Fans of the original will see almost nothing
familiar in the reboot.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:16.920
And younger audiences may be more familiar
with Brendan Fraser's Mummy trilogy anyway.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.270
Those movies emphasized campy fun and had
help selling its story with its 1920s setting,

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:26.110
which gave a period-appropriate feel to the
classic monster tale.

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:30.690
Cruise's version, meanwhile, takes place in
the present day, a move that arguably takes

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:34.970
the film even further from the classic adventure
story it's trying to be.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:36.200
Rehash fatigue

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:40.490
Hollywood has spent the last several years
turning to familiar properties audiences loved

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:41.490
in the past.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:46.890
But the string of flops from 2017's crop of
reimaginings suggests filmgoers might be getting

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.140
tired of all the modern revivals.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:54.150
King Arthur: Legend of the Sword, Dwayne "The
Rock" Johnson's Baywatch, and the fifth Pirates

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:57.690
of the Caribbean flick all fell far below
expectations.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:02.060
All of these films had something in common:
rotten reviews and bad word of mouth.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.460
As successful recent remakes, reboots, and
sequels like Beauty and the Beast and The

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:10.400
Fate of the Furious have shown, audiences
are only willing to turn out to see overly

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.489
familiar films if they're actually any good.

00:03:13.489 --> 00:03:14.730
Cart before horse

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:19.690
Universal's Dark Universe franchise is set
to include beloved characters such as the

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:24.569
Invisible Man, the Phantom of the Opera, and
Frankenstein, among many others.

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:25.790
"It's alive!

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:27.019
It's alive!

00:03:27.019 --> 00:03:28.250
It's alive!

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:29.470
It's alive!"

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:31.540
Well, let's not be too hasty, Doc.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:36.360
The studio may have killed the whole enterprise's
chances at success by announcing it before

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:41.040
even making a single successful film, hobbling
The Mummy and making its attempt at world-building

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.500
seem disingenuous to audiences.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:48.070
As many of the reviews pointed out, the movie
struggles with that world-building, leaving

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:53.090
the narrative feeling slow and labored, with
heavy exposition interrupting the action.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:57.519
Launching the Dark Universe right off the
bat without trying to first create a successful,

00:03:57.519 --> 00:04:01.940
fun film played a big role in The Mummy's
overcrowded feel, and could have a negative

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.280
effect on the rest of the franchise going
forward.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:06.280
Star power-outage

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.090
Internationally, big names like Tom Cruise
still sell movies.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:14.900
But at home, data shows that people just don't
care as much about a movie's star power as

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.360
they once did.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:21.230
Recent examples include Dwayne Johnson in
Baywatch and Tom Hanks in The Circle — high-profile

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:27.540
flops highlighted by the surprising performances
of 2017 sleeper hits like Get Out and Split,

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.940
neither of which had huge names attached.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:30.940
So what gives?

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:35.770
For one thing, more stars are moving to movies
on TV and streaming, with huge names like

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:41.190
Brad Pitt crafting films for Netflix and stars
like Matthew McConaughey and Nicole Kidman

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:45.390
making prestige TV like True Detective and
Big Little Lies.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:50.630
The number of films in theaters is also increasing,
meaning that viewers simply have more to choose

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:51.630
from.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:52.630
Buzz need

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:57.139
Marketing is important, but organic buzz is
what can really make a film blow up — and

00:04:57.139 --> 00:04:58.690
The Mummy never really had any.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:03.470
A Fandango poll looking for the most anticipated
film of the summer didn't even put it in the

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:08.050
top five, while a look at social media buzz
on Twitter showed that the film wasn't among

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:11.810
the top five most-tweeted-about movies the
months leading up to its release.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:16.750
The summer box office is overcrowded with
huge movies, and without positive word of

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:21.470
mouth, it's hard for one movie, even one with
an established name like The Mummy, to break

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:22.470
out.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:23.910
"What are you thinking?"

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.810
I'm thinking we're probably going to die here."

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:27.810
"I knew it!"

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:28.960
No wonder

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:32.910
Making matters worse, The Mummy faced one
of the toughest competitors of the summer

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:37.260
during its opening weekend: Wonder Woman,
the juggernaut that beat the box office with

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:41.530
an expectation-shattering $103 million opening.

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:46.069
Wonder Woman also used its stellar reviews
to earn the biggest second week for any film

00:05:46.069 --> 00:05:49.090
in the DC Extended Universe franchise thus
far.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:53.380
The Mummy likely couldn't have topped Wonder
Woman's second-week grosses even if it had

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.490
received positive reviews.

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.169
Salvaged overseas

00:05:57.169 --> 00:06:02.150
The box office market is very different overseas,
and it's provided a rescue for quite a few

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:04.460
domestic flops in recent years.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:08.810
This looks to be the case for The Mummy, which
gave Cruise the largest international opening

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:09.810
of his career.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:15.460
The film did best in Cruise-friendly territories
like China, South Korea, Russia, Mexico, and

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:19.150
Taiwan, where the star's name continues to
carry quite a lot of weight.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:21.240
"It's not an exact science, this business."

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:26.020
This may offer a lifeboat for the future of
the Dark Universe, which has a few other big-name

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:30.640
stars attached for upcoming movies, including
Johnny Depp as the Invisible Man.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.170
Still, The Mummy's scary lack of domestic
appeal adds a troubling wrinkle to the future

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:37.330
of Universal's monster movie initiative.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:38.960
Thanks for watching!

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.880
